{
  "spec": "Exact-decimal comparison contract (H-03). Both runtimes compare NUM_RE-shaped operands as decimal STRINGS - no float, no bcmath, no BigInt - because the IEEE-754 cast silently merged distinct values above 2^53 and truncated long fractions, so @UVASSERT=\"[id]=[id_confirm]\" accepted two different identifiers. Logic::decCmp in php/Logic.php and QRID_whenDecCmp in js/engine.js are the twins; tests/numeric_php.php and tests/numeric_js.cjs drive them over this file. Each case declares one comparison: \"cmp\" is -1/0/1 for a<b, a==b, a>b and the six verdicts are derived from it, so every op is pinned per case. \"path\" says which branch of compare() must be taken - \"numeric\" when BOTH trimmed operands are NUM_RE-shaped, \"string\" otherwise; string-path cases are re-checked against an independent byte/code-point comparison written in each test, which is what proves the engine really fell through. \"floatTrap\" marks the cases where the old float cast gave a DIFFERENT answer; each test recomputes the float verdict and fails if it agrees, so these stay genuine H-03 sentinels. \"digest\" is the sha256 of every verdict both runtimes produce, so PHP and JS cannot drift case-for-case even if someone edits one expectation. A \"mixed\" case has one NUM_RE operand and one not, with neither blank: ordering is undefined across domains, so < > <= >= are all false while = and <> still follow byte equality. Choosing the comparator per PAIR made ordering non-transitive (\"2\"<=\"10\", \"10\"<=\"1e1\", \"2\">\"1e1\" all true at once).",
  "ops": [
    "=",
    "<>",
    "<",
    ">",
    "<=",
    ">="
  ],
  "shapes": [
    "ref_ref",
    "ref_lit",
    "lit_ref",
    "lit_lit"
  ],
